---
/**
 * Compound interest calculator — 영국판 세 번째 도구. 한국어판 /tools/compound/ 와 계산(lib/compound.ts)·스크립트를 같이 쓰고
 * (문구는 scripts/compound.ts 의 EN 사전, 통화는 판의 GBP), 문장은 영국 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님). 복리 산술이라 rates 와 상관없다(조사표 영국 칸 '같음').
 *
 * 소제목은 영국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/gb-compound-2026-10-20.csv, hl en · gl gb):
 *   compound interest calculator uk monthly · regular savings calculator uk / savings calculator uk aer /
 *   compound interest calculator with inflation / compound interest calculator uk stocks and shares isa · pension / ... uk excel
 * 세율 기본값은 0% — 한국어판 15.4% 는 한국 원천징수라 쓰지 않는다. 영국 세금 숫자는 GOV.UK 에서 확인한 것만:
 *   'You can save tax-free with Individual Savings Accounts (ISAs)' · 2026 to 2027 ISA 한도 £20,000
 *   (https://www.gov.uk/individual-savings-accounts, 확인 2026-10-20). 그 밖의 세율·공제는 적지 않는다.
 * AER 환산(12 × [(1 + AER)^(1/12) − 1])은 산술이다. 본문 숫자는 전부 아래 frontmatter 에서 compound() 로 계산한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { compound } from "../../../../lib/compound";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";

const title = "Compound Interest Calculator UK — Monthly Savings, AER, Inflation";
const description =
  "UK compound interest calculator in pounds: a lump sum plus regular monthly savings, interest earned, AER conversion, inflation-adjusted value and Excel FV.";

const base = { principal: 1_000, monthly: 250, ratePct: 5, months: 120, taxPct: 0, inflationPct: 0 };
const ex = compound({ ...base, timing: "start" });
const exEnd = compound({ ...base, timing: "end" });
const lump = compound({ ...base, monthly: 0, timing: "start" });
const real3 = compound({ ...base, timing: "start", inflationPct: 3 });
/** 연 5% 를 12 로 나눠 매달 복리로 돌리면 실제 1년 이율(AER)은 얼마인가 */
const aerOf5 = (Math.pow(1 + 0.05 / 12, 12) - 1) * 100;
/** AER 5% 와 같은 결과를 내는 연 이율(매달 복리) */
const nominalFor5 = 12 * (Math.pow(1.05, 1 / 12) - 1) * 100;
const exAer = compound({ ...base, ratePct: Number(nominalFor5.toFixed(3)), timing: "start" });
const gbp = (x: number) =>
  Math.round(x).toLocaleString("en-GB", { style: "currency", currency: "GBP", maximumFractionDigits: 0, minimumFractionDigits: 0 });
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Compound Interest Calculator</h1>
      <p>
        Put in a starting balance, what you save each month and an interest rate or expected return. The calculator compounds the
        pot monthly and shows the final balance, how much of it you paid in and how much is interest. The rate, tax and inflation
        figures are your own assumptions — nothing here forecasts what a real account or fund will do.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-principal">Starting balance (£)</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="cp-principal" value="1,000" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-monthly">Monthly saving (£)</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="cp-monthly" value="250" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-rate">Interest rate or return a year</label>
          <input type="number" id="cp-rate" value="5" min="-50" max="100" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" />
          <span class="unit">%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-period">Length of time</label>
          <input type="number" id="cp-period" value="10" min="1" max="600" step="1" data-step />
          <select id="cp-unit" aria-label="Time unit">
            <option value="years" selected>years</option>
            <option value="months">months</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-timing">When you pay in</label>
          <select id="cp-timing" data-seg>
            <option value="start" selected>Start of each month</option>
            <option value="end">End of each month</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-tax">Tax on the interest (assumption)</label>
          <input type="number" id="cp-tax" value="0" min="0" max="100" step="0.001" inputmode="decimal" />
          <span class="unit">%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-infl">Inflation a year</label>
          <input type="number" id="cp-infl" value="0" min="-20" max="50" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" />
          <span class="unit">%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="cp-goal">Savings target (£, optional)</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="cp-goal" value="" placeholder="e.g. 40,000" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>

        <div
          class="result-hero"
          aria-live="polite"
          aria-atomic="true"
          data-bar="cp-paid|cp-gain2"
          data-bar-labels="Paid in|Interest"
          data-dock="cp-hero"
        >
          <p class="label">Final balance (before tax)</p>
          <p class="big" id="cp-hero">{gbp(ex.balance)}</p>
          <p class="meta" id="cp-note">120 months · 0.4167% a month · deposits at the start of each month</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">Total paid in</span><span class="value" id="cp-paid">{gbp(ex.paid)}</span></div>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">Interest earned</span><span class="value" id="cp-gain2">{gbp(ex.gain)}</span></div>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">Tax (assumed)</span><span class="value" id="cp-tax-v">£0</span></div>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">After tax</span><span class="value" id="cp-after">{gbp(ex.afterTax)}</span></div>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">In today's money</span><span class="value" id="cp-real">{gbp(ex.real)}</span></div>
          <div class="stat-chip"><span class="label">Target reached</span><span class="value" id="cp-goal-v">No goal set</span></div>
        </div>

        <details>
          <summary>Year-by-year payments, interest and balance</summary>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Paid in that year</th><th>Interest that year</th><th>Balance</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody id="cp-rows">
                {ex.years.map((r) => <tr><th scope="row">Year {r.year}</th><td>{gbp(r.paid)}</td><td>{gbp(r.gain)}</td><td>{gbp(r.balance)}</td></tr>)}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="meta">The starting balance is not counted in "Paid in that year"; it is only part of the balance.</p>
        </details>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Compound interest with monthly savings: how it is worked out</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monthly rate</strong> — r = yearly rate ÷ 12. At 5% a year that is about 0.4167% a month.</li>
        <li><strong>Paying in at the start of the month</strong> — the new money earns that month's interest: balance = (balance + saving) × (1 + r).</li>
        <li><strong>Paying in at the end of the month</strong> — interest goes on first, then the deposit: balance = balance × (1 + r) + saving. The last payment earns nothing, so the total is slightly lower.</li>
        <li><strong>Formula</strong> — after n months, FV = P(1 + r)<sup>n</sup> + PMT × [(1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1] ÷ r, with the PMT part multiplied by (1 + r) once more for start-of-month payments. P is the starting balance and PMT the monthly saving.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax</strong> — if the final balance is more than you paid in, the tax rate you enter is taken once from that gain. A loss is not taxed.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Example: {gbp(base.principal)} to start, {gbp(base.monthly)} a month at 5% a year for 10 years. You pay in {gbp(ex.paid)}{" "}
        and finish with {gbp(ex.balance)}, of which {gbp(ex.gain)} is interest. Paying in at the end of each month instead gives
        {" "}{gbp(exEnd.balance)}.
      </p>

      <h2>Regular savings vs a lump sum</h2>
      <p>
        Money paid in on day one compounds for the whole term; each monthly payment has less time left to grow. In the example the
        {" "}{gbp(base.principal)} starting balance on its own would be {gbp(lump.balance)} after 10 years. Set the monthly saving to £0
        for a lump sum only, or the starting balance to £0 for a regular saver only.
      </p>

      <h2>Savings rates quoted as AER</h2>
      <p>
        UK savings accounts usually quote an AER (annual equivalent rate), which already includes the effect of interest being paid
        and compounded during the year. This calculator divides the yearly rate by 12 and compounds every month, so typing 5% here
        works out at about {aerOf5.toFixed(2)}% over a full year — a little more than a 5% AER. To match a 5% AER exactly, enter
        12 × [(1 + 0.05)<sup>1/12</sup> − 1] ≈ {nominalFor5.toFixed(3)}%. In the example that gives {gbp(exAer.balance)} instead of
        {" "}{gbp(ex.balance)}.
      </p>

      <h2>Compound interest calculator with inflation</h2>
      <p>
        The "In today's money" figure divides the after-tax balance by (1 + inflation)<sup>years</sup>. With 3% inflation the
        example's {gbp(ex.balance)} is worth about {gbp(real3.real)} in today's pounds. Leave inflation at 0% to see the cash
        figure only.
      </p>

      <h2>Compound interest formula in Excel</h2>
      <p>
        The FV function gives the same answer. For start-of-month payments use
        <code>=FV(5%/12, 120, -250, -1000, 1)</code>; for end-of-month payments change the last 1 to 0. The saving and the starting
        balance are negative because they are money going out. Google Sheets uses the same formula.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I use it for a stocks and shares ISA?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes, as long as you enter an assumed return rather than a guaranteed one — investments can fall as well as rise. GOV.UK
          says you can save tax-free with Individual Savings Accounts, so the default tax rate of 0% suits money held inside an ISA.
          The calculator does not stop you going over the yearly ISA limit, which GOV.UK gives as £20,000 for the 2026 to 2027 tax
          year (<a href="https://www.gov.uk/individual-savings-accounts" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Individual Savings Accounts</a>,
          checked 20 October 2026).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What about savings outside an ISA?</summary>
        <p>
          Whether you pay tax on savings interest depends on your other income and allowances, so the calculator does not choose a
          rate. Enter your own assumption, or 0% if no tax is due. GOV.UK explains the rules in
          <a href="https://www.gov.uk/apply-tax-free-interest-on-savings" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Tax on savings interest</a>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Does it work as a pension calculator?</summary>
        <p>
          It shows how a steady monthly amount grows at an assumed return. It does not add tax relief, employer contributions, pay
          rises, charges or withdrawals — if your employer pays in too, add their monthly amount to yours.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I enter pence or a £ sign?</summary>
        <p>
          Amounts are whole pounds. A typed £ sign and commas are ignored, but a decimal point is not read as pence — enter 1500
          rather than 1,500.50.
        </p>
      </details>

      <AdSlot label="compound-result-en-gb" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/compound.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
